  "status": "Approved",
  "description": "1. Description of Proposed Development\nInstallation of a rooftop mounted solar photovoltaic (PV) system with a total capacity of approximately 20 kWp (10kWp per unit) (comprising 46 x 450W panels) on the roofs of Unit 5 & 6, South Cargo, Girling Way, Heathrow, TW14 0PH\nThe proposal comprises the installation of solar PV panels mounted to the existing roof structure, together with associated mounting frames, cabling, inverter equipment and ancillary electrical infrastructure.\nNo external alterations to the building are proposed beyond the installation of the solar PV equipment.\n2. Compliance with Schedule 2, Part 14, Class J\nThis submission has been prepared with regard to Schedule 2, Part 14, Class J of the Town and Country Planning (General Permitted Development) (England) Order 2015 (as amended).\nThe proposed development accords with the relevant limitations and conditions of Class J, including:\nThe solar equipment will not protrude more than 200mm beyond the plane of the roof slope.\nThe highest part of the solar equipment will not exceed the highest part of the existing roof (excluding chimneys).\nThe equipment will be sited so far as practicable to minimise its effect on the external appearance of the building and the amenity of the area.\nThe installation will be removed as soon as reasonably practicable when no longer required.\nThe layout has been arranged to respect appropriate setback distances from roof edges and rooflights, including:\nA minimum setback of 1.5m from building edges.\nA minimum clearance of 1.2m from skylights and roof penetrations.\nThe system has been designed so that it does not exceed the structural load capacity of the existing roof.\n3. Visual and Amenity Considerations\nThe solar panels to be installed are dark blue/black in colour, incorporate anti-glare coating and are non-reflective in nature.\nThe panels are static, contain no moving parts and will not generate noise. The development will not materially alter the appearance of the building or restrict its use in any way.\nThe proposal has been developed to minimise visual impact on the building and surrounding area.\n4. Environmental Benefits\nThe installation of the solar PV system will reduce carbon emissions associated with the building and contribute to the decarbonisation of the site’s operational energy use.\nThe system will be installed by a government certified (MCS) solar PV engineering contractor with experience in delivering comparable commercial rooftop installations.\n5. Submission Requirements\nIn accordance with the requirements of Class J, this submission includes:\nA written description of the proposed development\nA plan indicating the site and showing the proposed development\nThe developer’s contact address\nThe developer’s email address for electronic correspondence",
  "decision_conditions": [
    "Condition : 1 - The solar PV equipment or solar thermal equipment shall not be installed within 1 metre of the external edge of the roof.\r\nReason: In order to comply with Article 3 of the Town and Country Planning (General Permitted Development) (England) Order 2015 (as amended).",
    "Condition : 2 - 2)\tThe solar PV equipment or solar thermal equipment shall not protrude more than 0.2 metres beyond the plane of the roof slope when measured from the perpendicular with the external surface of the roof slope.\r\nReason: In order to comply with Article 3 of the Town and Country Planning (General Permitted Development) (England) Order 2015 (as amended).",
    "Condition : 3 - 3)\tThe solar PV equipment or solar thermal equipment must, so far as practicable, be sited so as to minimise its effect on the external appearance of the building and the amenity of the area.\r\nReason: In order to comply with Article 3 of the Town and Country Planning (General Permitted Development) (England) Order 2015 (as amended).",
    "Condition : 4 - 4)\tThe solar PV equipment or solar thermal equipment is removed as soon as reasonably practicable when no longer needed.\r\nReason: In order to comply with Article 3 of the Town and Country Planning (General Permitted Development) (England) Order 2015 (as amended)."
